        data_center_notes: "UPDATED 2026-08-20: CAL Realty, LLC and Raeden Acquisitions, LLC filed suit against the City of Gibraltar (Case No. 2:26-cv-12893-RJW-APP, U.S. District Court, E.D. Mich., filed Aug 17, 2026 — counsel: Honigman LLP for CAL Realty, Bodman PLC for Raeden). Complaint alleges the city's Mar 9, 2026 one-year data center moratorium is an unlawful 'blanket prohibition' and that the city has refused to consider Raeden's site plan for a ~100MW data center at the former McLouth Steel site (27800 W. Jefferson Ave, a capped Superfund property) — a use plaintiffs say is permitted by right, would self-generate most of its power, and would use closed-loop cooling. This is the second Michigan data-center-moratorium lawsuit in 10 days after Wixom's (see Wixom entry). Raeden Co-Founder/COO: Jason Green. Local opposition: Indivisible: Downriver United 734. UPDATE 2026-09-10 (sweep): no docket movement or city response found publicly as of this date. Source: Law360 Real Estate Authority, Aug 18 2026; Planet Detroit, Mar 2026 — https://planetdetroit.org/2026/03/gibraltar-data-center-moratorium/ UPDATE 2026-09-10 (Wayne County doc audit): the city's own documents-on-demand.com meeting-document portal remains inaccessible to automated tools (confirmed blocked across three separate methods this session) -- Mo pulled Council/PC agendas, minutes, and packets herself directly from that portal. One corroborating secondary source found: Wayne County's regional 'Wayne Forward' program published a Data Centers FAQ (dated Mar 18, 2026) that explicitly references Gibraltar's one-year moratorium and confirms the McLouth Steel site is the same project as the Raeden 100MW proposal. UPDATE 2026-09-10 (part 2, Mo's own primary-source pull -- Gibraltar PC packets, Aug 18 & Sept 8 2026): the city is running a comprehensive rewrite of its entire Zoning Ordinance (Chapter 44), Final Draft dated Aug 25, 2026, with a Sept 8, 2026 Planning Commission special meeting/public hearing to recommend adoption to City Council. The new draft adds a general Section 5.12 'Moratoria' provision codifying substantive/procedural findings-based standards for any future moratorium (6-month initial cap, findings of fact required, uniform application) -- directly relevant to Raeden/CAL Realty's pending 'unlawful blanket prohibition' claim over the existing data-center moratorium. ALSO FOUND: an Aug 28, 2026 public-comment letter from resident Anthony Arminiak to the Mayor/Council/PC requests adding 'Digital Storage Warehouse' as a permitted use in the M-2 General Manufacturing District, specifically citing 'the data center ordinance language...prepared by McKenna and presented at the April 21, 2026 Planning Commission meeting' -- confirms the city's own contracted firm (McKenna, Chuck Earl's firm) had already drafted a data-center-specific ordinance months before the Raeden lawsuit was filed (Aug 17, 2026), though it does not appear to have been adopted; as of the Aug 25 Final Draft, data centers/digital storage warehouses are NOT listed as a permitted M-2 use. Worth watching whether this ordinance rewrite (or a future amendment) adds it. Sources: Wayne-Forward-Data-Centers-FAQ.pdf (gibraltarcitymi.gov); City of Gibraltar Planning Commission packets, Aug 18 & Sept 8, 2026 (Mo's own downloads).",
