        data_center_case: true,
        data_center_notes: "MAJOR CASE — an operating hyperscale data center/crypto-mining facility (Alliance Cloud Services, LLC, a subsidiary of Hyperscale Data, Inc., CEO Milton \"Todd\" Ault III) has run since 2022 at the Business Center of Southwest Michigan. In 2026 the company announced expansion from ~30 MW to 340 MW by 2029, and from 34.5 to 83+ acres. Timeline: Jan 7, 2026 City Manager Kevin Anderson publicly addresses expansion concerns (no formal application filed yet; power currently from AEP, not city utilities); ~Jan 13, 2026 City Council denies a 100+-signature resident petition for a moratorium (Mayor Patrick Bakeman opposed pending an actual site plan); Mar 2, 2026 PC hearing on a rezoning at 414 West Street tied to the facility, recommended approval; Mar 23, 2026 Council packet includes Ordinance 2026-01, a new noise ordinance (Residential 65 dBA day/55 night; Commercial 65; Industrial 75) drafted explicitly in response to the expansion; Apr 1, 2026 Mayor Bakeman sends an open letter to Hyperscale Data CEO Will Horne demanding disclosure of the target property and a 45-day plan; by May 31, 2026 residents file a class-action lawsuit alleging unreasonable/excessive noise (60-70 dBA at property line, described as constant vacuum-cleaner/dishwasher-level noise); Jul 29, 2026 organizers hold a meeting explicitly aiming to unite all 15 Cass County townships on zoning protections and community benefit agreements. Indiana Michigan Power (AEP) has publicly stated it has no agreements/commitments for the claimed 300 MW expansion — some company claims are utility-disputed. No moratorium was ultimately adopted by the City itself (petition denied) — contrast with Jefferson/Penn/Porter Townships (ids 520/527/529), which did adopt township-level moratoria in the same window. Primary document: City Council packet PDF containing Ordinance 2026-01 (noise ordinance). UPDATE 2026-08-21 (30-day sweep): parent Hyperscale Data, Inc.'s board launched a formal review of strategic alternatives including a POSSIBLE SALE OR LEASE of part or all of the Dowagiac facility (Herald-Palladium, Aug 12-14, 2026) — Executive Chairman Milton \"Todd\" Ault III cites a ~$1.2B contract value, potentially $4B+ with the announced expansion. Significant new wrinkle mid-expansion and mid-class-action-lawsuit: a sale/lease could change both the litigation posture and the pace of the 30MW→340MW buildout. Separately, the city held a resident lawsuit-update meeting Jul 1, 2026, and Hyperscale's CEO addressed council publicly for the first time Jul 14, 2026 amid ongoing noise complaints (Watershed Voice). UPDATE 2026-09-10 (sweep): Sept 1, 2026 — Hyperscale Data halted ALL Bitcoin-mining operations at the facility, freeing power/infrastructure capacity for AI computing (currently 30MW, still targeting 340MW). Disclosed a new customer: an unnamed California-based 'neocloud' company will deploy 20MW by Q4 2026 (scaling to 52MW) under a 10-year, ~$1.2B master services agreement with two 5-year extension options. The board's Aug 11 strategic-alternatives review (possible sale/lease) remains open with no timetable or conclusion announced as of this date; no new developments found on the noise class-action. Source: DataCenterDynamics, Sept 3, 2026. UPDATE 2026-09-10 (30-day BESS/DC sweep, additional detail): total contract value reported as scaling to as much as $3B with the neocloud customer\u0027s expansion options exercised (vs. the initial $1.2B figure) -- per Cryptonomist, Sept 2, 2026.",
        notes: "RESEARCH PASS 2026-08-06 (primary-source sweep of Cass/St. Joseph/Kalamazoo counties): This is the single most significant data-center data point across this entire sweep — an existing, operating, actively-expanding facility rather than a proposed one. Wightman (gowightman.com) provided a discrete zoning/GIS online-mapping project for the city, described by Wightman itself as a project partnership rather than an ongoing planning-consultant retainer — not treating Wightman as the city's current planning firm; Steve Allen (in-house Zoning Administrator) is the confirmed current role.",
